+# Filtering the Veterinarian Directory by Specialty
+
+**Status:** Accepted
+
+## Context
+
+NG-9 declined searching for a pet, a visit, or a veterinarian, leaving only owners searchable. A request now asks to narrow the veterinarian side: filter the directory by a specialty it already displays. This sits against the NG-9 boundary — a filter on a shown attribute reads differently from the free-text search NG-9 declined, and the boundary has to say which side it lands on. The request also reopens the machine-readable veterinarian list, withdrawn as `REQ-VET-002` (an implementation artifact no consumer used), by making that surface the filter's first supported capability.
+
+The owner has decided both points (2026-08-26).
+
+## Options Considered
+
+1. **Read NG-9 as covering all veterinarian narrowing.** Rejected: it conflates free-text search — matching typed text against records — with filtering a listing by an attribute already on the page. The second is bounded and demonstrates a distinct pattern.
+2. **Open free-text veterinarian search.** Rejected: the NG-9 rationale holds — repeating owner search's paged prefix match for another entity adds surface, not understanding.
+3. **Narrow NG-9 to admit specialty filtering while free-text search stays out** (chosen), and reinstate the machine-readable list as a supported surface under a fresh requirement id.
+
+## Decision
+
+Filtering the veterinarian directory by a whole, case-insensitive specialty name is in scope on both the page and the machine-readable surface (`REQ-VET-003`, `REQ-VET-004`). Free-text veterinarian search stays out of scope; NG-9 keeps that half. The machine-readable list is a supported surface again under `REQ-VET-004`; the withdrawn `REQ-VET-002` stays withdrawn and its id is not reused.
+
+The filter is a URL contract only in this scope — neither surface gains a form, menu, or other on-page control; the directory's paging links carry the specialty. A visible control may come as a separate request.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- NG-9 is narrowed, not lifted: the directory gains one bounded filter, and free-text veterinarian search remains declined.
+- A previously withdrawn surface returns under a new id, so links to the old id still resolve to the Superseded list while the capability lives at `REQ-VET-004`.
+- Whether surrounding whitespace on a non-empty filter value should be trimmed to mirror owner search is left open; the narrowest reading (no trimming) is taken for this slice and recorded in the PRD Open Questions.
+
+## Implementation
+
+**Non-goal:** NG-9
+
+- [PRD Non-Goals](../prd.md#non-goals) — the narrowed NG-9 row.
+- [PRD Veterinarian directory](../prd.md#req-vet-003) — `REQ-VET-003` and `REQ-VET-004`, the in-scope filter capabilities.
